---
import PSEO from '@/layouts/PSEO.astro';
import { buildArticleSchema, buildFAQSchema } from '@/lib/schema';
import {
  APP_NAME,
  SITE_URL,
  AUTHOR_NAME,
  AUTHOR_URL,
  OSS_REPO_PUBLIC,
  OSS_REPO_URL,
} from '@/constants';
import { PLANS } from '@/data/pricing';

// Targets the `open source alternative to outrank` / `open source seo tool`
// cluster: an unclaimed category, and reachable at DR 0.2 in a way the head
// terms are not. See memory/hot-cache.md.
//
// IMPORTANT: every claim on this page that depends on the repo actually being
// public is gated on OSS_REPO_PUBLIC (@/constants). While it is false the page
// states the status plainly rather than implying you can clone it today.
// Asserting an unavailable license is the same class of claim as fabricated
// traction, see the deleted Results block in PRODUCT.md.

const pageUrl = `${SITE_URL}/open-source`;

const publishDate = new Date('2026-08-15');
const dateModified = new Date('2026-08-15');
const publishDateDisplay = publishDate.toLocaleDateString('en-US', {
  year: 'numeric',
  month: 'long',
  day: 'numeric',
});

const breadcrumbs = [
  { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
  { label: 'Open source', href: '/open-source' },
];

// What "no feature gates" actually means, stated as the things competitors gate.
const NOT_GATED = [
  {
    feature: 'Multi-client workspaces',
    typical: 'Reserved for an agency or enterprise tier',
    ours: 'In the free self-hosted build',
  },
  {
    feature: 'White-label reports',
    typical: 'Premium add-on, or locked to the top tier',
    ours: 'In the free self-hosted build',
  },
  {
    feature: 'All 11 CMS integrations',
    typical: 'Metered by connection count',
    ours: 'In the free self-hosted build',
  },
  {
    feature: 'Editorial approval workflow',
    typical: 'Often absent entirely, autopilot by default',
    ours: 'In the free self-hosted build',
  },
  {
    feature: 'The SEO scoring algorithm',
    typical: 'A black-box number you cannot inspect',
    ours: 'Readable source you can audit and change',
  },
];

const FAQS = [
  {
    question: `Is ${APP_NAME} really open source?`,
    answer: OSS_REPO_PUBLIC
      ? `Yes. ${APP_NAME} is open source, and the whole product is in the public repository: agency multi-tenant, white-label reporting, the approval workflow, and all 11 CMS adapters. There is no separate "enterprise" directory and no paid tier held back from the source.`
      : `That is the plan, and the decision is locked, but not yet. The repository is not public at the time of writing. ${APP_NAME} will be open source with the whole product in the open: agency multi-tenant, white-label reporting, the approval workflow, and all 11 CMS adapters. No separate "enterprise" directory, no paid tier held back. This page will link to the repository the day it goes public.`,
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I run this for my own clients, commercially?',
    answer:
      'Yes, and for free. Self-host it, run it as-is for your clients, and you owe nothing and publish nothing: commercial use on behalf of clients is exactly what the licence is for. One obligation exists and it only reaches people rebuilding the product, not people using it. If you modify the source and offer that modified version to others over a network, your changes have to be shared back on the same terms. Running an agency on it is free and unencumbered. Relaunching a modified copy as a competing service is not.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What stops a competitor taking it and reselling it?',
    answer:
      'Nothing stops anyone self-hosting or forking it, and that is deliberate. What the licence does stop is a competitor taking the multi-client workspaces, the white-label reporting and the exchange, changing them, and running the result as a closed commercial service without sharing those changes back. That fence is the reason the whole product can ship in the open instead of holding features back in a paid tier: what is defensible here is software, not a metered API.',
  },
  {
    question: 'If I can self-host it for free, what am I paying for?',
    answer:
      'Hosting, the model bill, and not maintaining it. The self-hosted build is the same software, but you supply the infrastructure and your own API keys, and you carry the upgrades. The paid tiers exist because someone else runs it, the model and data costs are included, and the output limits are metered. Nothing is withheld from the free build to make the paid one attractive.',
  },
  {
    question: `Can I white-label ${APP_NAME} for my clients?`,
    answer: `Yes, and it is in the free self-hosted build rather than gated behind a premium tier. That is the point of the model for agencies: run it on your own infrastructure, put your own brand on the reports and the client portal, and keep both the client relationship and the margin. Your clients never need to know what is underneath.`,
  },
  {
    question: 'What ships first?',
    answer:
      'It already has. The repository is public and it is the whole product, not a stripped first slice: the engine, the dashboard, agency multi-tenant, white-label reporting, the approval gate and all 11 CMS adapters. An MCP server is included, so an AI assistant can research and draft against your own brief. The one thing that is NOT built yet is a packaged command-line tool; the plan was to lead with one, and shipping everything at once turned out to be the smaller step.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does the approval gate survive in the open-source build?',
    answer:
      'Yes, and it is not optional. Nothing publishes without a human approving it, and the gate lives in the publish step itself so scheduled jobs and bulk actions cannot route around it. Publishing defaults to draft status. This is the deliberate opposite of autopilot tools, and it is the behaviour most likely to keep you out of trouble on a client brand.',
  },
];

const structuredData = [
  buildArticleSchema({
    title: `Open Source AI SEO Content Engine, ${APP_NAME}`,
    description:
      'AltoRank is going open source, the whole product, with no feature gates. What that means for agencies, what you can and cannot do with it, and what ships first.',
    publishDate,
    dateModified,
    author: AUTHOR_NAME,
    authorType: 'Person',
    authorUrl: AUTHOR_URL,
    url: pageUrl,
  }),
  buildFAQSchema(FAQS),
];
---

<PSEO
  title="Open Source SEO Tools: One Self-Hosted Content Engine"
  description="AltoRank is going open source, the whole product, no feature gates. What that means for you, what stops a competitor reselling it, and what ships first."
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  structuredData={structuredData}
>
  {/* ── HERO ──────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <header class="border-b border-line bg-[radial-gradient(600px_300px_at_88%_-10%,var(--accent-soft),transparent_70%),linear-gradient(180deg,var(--bg),var(--bg-2))] py-[72px]">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-[760px] px-8">
      <h1 class="mb-6 text-[clamp(34px,5vw,48px)] font-semibold leading-[1.05] tracking-[-0.025em] text-ink [text-wrap:balance]">
        Open source SEO tools, in one self-hosted content engine
      </h1>
      <p class="mb-5 text-lg font-semibold leading-relaxed text-ink [text-wrap:pretty]">
        {APP_NAME} is an open-source alternative to Outrank and Jasper: research a keyword,
        generate the brief, write the article, score it, and publish it to any of 11 CMS
        platforms. Open source, with the whole product in the open:
        agency multi-tenant, white-label reporting, and the editorial approval gate included,
        not held back for a paid tier. Self-host it for nothing, or let us run it.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-7 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-3 gap-y-1 border-t border-line-soft pt-5 text-[13px] text-ink-3">
        <span>By <a href={AUTHOR_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="font-medium text-ink-2 underline decoration-line decoration-1 underline-offset-[3px] transition-colors hover:text-ink hover:decoration-accent">{AUTHOR_NAME}</a></span>
        <span class="text-line">·</span>
        <span>Published <time datetime={publishDate.toISOString().slice(0, 10)}>{publishDateDisplay}</time></span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  {/* ── STATUS ────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  {/* Honest pre-launch status. Disappears automatically once the repo is public. */}
  <section class="border-b border-line py-10">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-[760px] px-8">
      {OSS_REPO_PUBLIC ? (
        <a
          href={OSS_REPO_URL}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          class="flex items-center gap-3 rounded-xl border border-ink bg-ink px-5 py-4 text-bg transition-opacity hover:opacity-90"
        >
          <svg class="h-5 w-5 shrink-0" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M12 .5C5.73.5.5 5.73.5 12c0 5.08 3.29 9.39 7.86 10.91.58.11.79-.25.79-.56v-2c-3.2.7-3.88-1.54-3.88-1.54-.53-1.34-1.29-1.7-1.29-1.7-1.05-.72.08-.7.08-.7 1.16.08 1.77 1.19 1.77 1.19 1.03 1.77 2.7 1.26 3.36.96.1-.75.4-1.26.73-1.55-2.55-.29-5.23-1.28-5.23-5.68 0-1.26.45-2.28 1.19-3.09-.12-.29-.52-1.46.11-3.05 0 0 .97-.31 3.18 1.18a11 11 0 0 1 5.8 0c2.2-1.49 3.17-1.18 3.17-1.18.63 1.59.23 2.76.12 3.05.74.81 1.18 1.83 1.18 3.09 0 4.41-2.68 5.38-5.24 5.67.41.35.78 1.05.78 2.12v3.14c0 .31.21.68.8.56A11.51 11.51 0 0 0 23.5 12C23.5 5.73 18.27.5 12 .5Z" />
          </svg>
          <span class="text-[14.5px] font-medium">View the source on GitHub &rarr;</span>
        </a>
      ) : (
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel px-5 py-4">
          <div class="mb-1.5 flex items-center gap-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.08em] text-warn">
            <span class="h-1.5 w-1.5 rounded-full bg-current"></span>
            Not public yet
          </div>
          <p class="text-[14.5px] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
            The repository is not open to the public at the time of writing, so there is
            nothing to clone today. The licence and the scope below are decided, not
            aspirational, but we would rather say that plainly than imply a download
            link exists. This page links to the source the day it goes public.
          </p>
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ── NO FEATURE GATES ──────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="border-b border-line py-[72px]">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-[760px] px-8">
      <div class="mb-8">
        <h2 class="mb-4 text-[clamp(24px,3.2vw,30px)] font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink [text-wrap:balance]">
          No <code class="rounded bg-panel-2 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[0.85em] text-ink-2">ee/</code> directory, no held-back tier
        </h2>
        <p class="text-[15.5px] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
          "Open core" usually means the interesting parts are the paid parts. Here the
          features people actually buy a tool for are in the free build. What you pay
          for is hosting, the model bill, and not being the one who maintains it.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full border-collapse text-[14px]">
          <caption class="sr-only">
            Features typically gated behind paid tiers, compared with what ships in the free self-hosted AltoRank build
          </caption>
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-line text-left">
              <th scope="col" class="py-2.5 pr-4 font-semibold text-ink-2">Capability</th>
              <th scope="col" class="py-2.5 pr-4 font-semibold text-ink-2">Typically</th>
              <th scope="col" class="py-2.5 font-semibold text-ink-2">In the free build</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {NOT_GATED.map((row) => (
              <tr class="border-b border-line-soft last:border-b-0">
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-ink">{row.feature}</td>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 text-ink-3">{row.typical}</td>
                <td class="py-3 font-medium text-ink-2">{row.ours}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ── FOR AGENCIES (the reseller framing) ───────────── */}
  <section class="border-b border-line py-[72px]">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-[760px] px-8">
      <div class="relative overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-ink bg-ink p-8 text-bg">
        <div class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 bg-[radial-gradient(500px_240px_at_100%_0%,oklch(0.45_0.15_265/0.45),transparent_60%)]"></div>
        <div class="relative z-10">
          <h2 class="mb-4 text-[clamp(23px,3vw,29px)] font-semibold leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] [text-wrap:balance]">
            Run it yourself. Put your brand on it. Keep the margin.
          </h2>
          <div class="flex flex-col gap-4 text-[15.5px] leading-relaxed text-[oklch(0.84_0.01_80)]">
            <p>
              The managed-service providers moving into AI search will happily sell your
              client an outcome directly, and cut you out of the relationship while
              they do it. The open-source build is the other side of that trade: the same
              execution layer, running on your infrastructure, under your brand.
            </p>
            <p>
              White-label reporting and multi-client workspaces are in the free build
              specifically so anyone can stand this up without a licence conversation.
              You own the client relationship, you set the retainer, and the software cost
              is your infrastructure bill rather than a per-seat line item that scales
              against you.
            </p>
            <p class="text-[oklch(0.9_0.01_80)]">
              Self-hosting it for your own clients carries no publication obligation, see the licence question below.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ── AUDITABLE MEASUREMENT ─────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="border-b border-line py-[72px]">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-[760px] px-8 flex flex-col gap-4 text-[15.5px] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
      <h2 class="mb-1 text-[clamp(24px,3.2vw,30px)] font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink [text-wrap:balance]">
        Ask three AI-visibility tools the same question, get three answers
      </h2>
      <p>
        This is the standing complaint about the entire AI-visibility category, and it is a
        fair one. Point several tools at the same brand with the same prompts and the
        citation rates disagree. Because every vendor's methodology is private, there is no
        way to tell which one is closer to the truth. You are asked to trust a number
        whose derivation you cannot see.
      </p>
      <p>
        Open source is the only real answer to that. When the prompt set, the sampling
        method, and the scoring algorithm are readable, a disagreement between two tools
        becomes a question you can settle by reading code instead of a matter of vendor
        faith. {OSS_REPO_PUBLIC
          ? 'Ours are in the repository. Read them, disagree with them, and open an issue if the methodology is wrong.'
          : 'Ours will be in the repository when it opens, including the scoring checks and the citation-tracking methodology. Until then the method is written up in plain language rather than asserted as a number.'}
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/geo/track-ai-citations-for-clients" class="font-medium text-accent-ink underline decoration-line decoration-1 underline-offset-[3px] transition-colors hover:decoration-accent">
          How we track AI citations for clients &rarr;
        </a>
        <span class="text-ink-3">: the frozen prompt set, logged-out sampling, and reporting cadence, written out in full.</span>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ── THE LADDER ────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="border-b border-line py-[72px]">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-[760px] px-8">
      <div class="mb-8">
        <h2 class="text-[clamp(24px,3.2vw,30px)] font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink [text-wrap:balance]">
          Free to self-host, priced when we run it
        </h2>
      </div>
      <div class="grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
        {PLANS.map((plan) => (
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-line bg-bg p-5">
            <div class="mb-1 flex items-baseline gap-1.5">
              <span class="text-[26px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.025em] leading-none text-ink">{plan.price}</span>
              <span class="text-[13px] text-ink-3">{plan.period}</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="mb-2 text-[15px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.015em] text-ink">{plan.name}</h3>
            <p class="text-[13.5px] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">{plan.desc}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p class="mt-5 text-[13.5px] text-ink-3">
        Full breakdown on the <a href="/pricing" class="font-medium text-accent-ink underline decoration-line decoration-1 underline-offset-[3px] hover:decoration-accent">pricing page</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ── WHAT SHIPS FIRST ──────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="border-b border-line py-[72px]">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-[760px] px-8 flex flex-col gap-4 text-[15.5px] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
      <h2 class="mb-1 text-[clamp(24px,3.2vw,30px)] font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink [text-wrap:balance]">
        The whole engine, in the open
      </h2>
      <p>
The open release is the whole product rather than a first slice: research a keyword,
        build a brief, write the article, score it against the on-page checks, lint it for
        the filler phrasing that marks generated text, and publish it to your CMS, plus the
        dashboard, agency multi-tenant and white-label reporting. It also runs from an AI
        assistant over MCP, point Claude Code at it and it can research and draft against
        your own brief. A packaged command-line tool is not built yet.
      </p>
      <p>
        The minimum to do useful work is a single Anthropic key. Keyword volume and SERP
        data need a DataForSEO key on top, and without one the brief step degrades to
        prompt-only rather than failing. Publishing defaults to draft status, always.
      </p>
      <p class="text-ink-3">
        The hosted dashboard, agency reporting, and billing come after. Saying so up front
        is more useful than a roadmap that implies everything lands at once.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ── FAQ ───────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="border-b border-line py-[72px]">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-[760px] px-8">
      <div class="mb-8">
        <h2 class="text-[clamp(24px,3.2vw,30px)] font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink [text-wrap:balance]">
          Common questions about the licence
        </h2>
      </div>
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-3">
        {FAQS.map((faq) => (
          <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-bg p-5 open:bg-panel">
            <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none text-[15.5px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] text-ink marker:hidden">
              {faq.question}
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-3 text-[14.5px] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">{faq.answer}</p>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p class="mt-6 text-[13px] leading-relaxed text-ink-3">
        This page describes how the licence is intended to work and is not legal advice.
        The full licence text ships with the source, and if the boundary matters to your business, take your own advice.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</PSEO>
